# The twelve-bar blues: I, IV and V in the order I I I I, IV IV I I, V IV I I, read from a chart in
# numerals and in chords, played on the ukulele in C and the guitar in A and G, with its three lines
# of words, at the waterfall gorge. W. C. Handy's St. Louis Blues (1914) is named as its source.
lesson music-twelve-bar-blues v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The twelve-bar blues"
  goal "Play the twelve-bar blues on the ukulele or guitar, changing between chords I, IV and V where its chart says."

  look {
    scene 36x25 {
      row top space=2 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        table chart cols=5 head=["Bars", "1st", "2nd", "3rd", "4th"] cells=["1 to 4", "I", "I", "I", "I", "5 to 8", "IV", "IV", "I", "I", "9 to 12", "V", "IV", "I", "I"]
        kingfisher bird flying=0 facing=-1
      }
      row boxes space=2 align=start below=top gap=1 {
        chordbox c tuning=uke chord=C frets=3 show=fingers name=true letters=false lit=[]
        chordbox f tuning=uke chord=F frets=3 show=fingers name=true letters=false lit=[]
        chordbox g tuning=uke chord=G7 frets=3 show=fingers name=true letters=false lit=[]
      }
    }
    say """
      By the water wheel the gorge band plays the blues, the music that grew up in the American
      South a little over a hundred years ago; W. C. Handy printed the St. Louis Blues in 1914. The
      most common blues uses twelve bars in three lines of four: four bars of chord I, two of IV and
      two of I, then V, IV, I and I. On the ukulele in C major that is C, F and G7.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.blues-count-bars count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    level easy {
      worked music.blues-missing-bar k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice music.blues-missing-bar count=1 seed=10 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=1
    practice music.blues-uke-strum count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.blues-changes count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.blues-guitar-strum count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.blues-words-lines count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.blues-play-roots count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    level hard {
      show music.blues-claim q=1 r=1
    }
    practice music.gorge-bass-notes count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.blues-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.blues-open-keys v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "The twelve-bar blues is I I I I, IV IV I I, V IV I I, and chord I has eight of the bars."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that the twelve-bar blues is one fixed plan that works in any key, so a player who
    knows I, IV and V in a key can play it, and that the words fit the plan in three lines, the
    first sung twice and the third answering it. The wrong ideas to watch for are thinking the bars
    are shared evenly between the chords, and changing chord a bar late in the last line, where it
    changes every bar. In the working, look for the child saying the numerals aloud in time before
    playing. The two-star question is the claim that each chord gets four bars; the three-star
    question checks a list of keys against the open guitar shapes a child already knows, which means
    finding I, IV and V in every key and rejecting any that needs F, B or B flat. A child who is
    stuck can say the plan as a chant, four, two, two, then one each, and go back to the way in;
    stay on this lesson another day if the last line still comes out wrong. Playing a whole blues in
    time is for you to judge in the room. Off the page, listen to a recording of the St. Louis Blues
    or any twelve-bar blues and count the bars together, raising a hand at bar 5 and bar 9. W. C.
    Handy died in 1958, so the St. Louis Blues is public domain in the United States but stays in
    copyright in the UK and the EU until 2029; the lesson names it and quotes none of it.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the bars to count are of I or IV, finding a missing bar is worked once in the teacher's pen before one in C major, the bars to strum are early in the blues in C and A, the changes are counted for one time through, the lines of words are found by where they start, the bass notes are counted for chord V, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star checks three keys."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the bars to count include those that are not I, the missing bars are in G or A on the guitar, the bars to strum are in the last line or in G major, the changes are counted with bar 12 turned to V, the chord of the answer line is read, the line starts are played in F, with its B flat, or in A, a claim that the chord changes only at a new line is judged before the two-star, the bass notes are counted over three times through, and the three-star checks all seven keys."
  }
}
